<header><p class="st-kicker">colony · Mesoamerica and the Caribbean</p><h1 class="st-title">British occupation of Havana</h1><p class="st-meta">1762 CE – 1763 CE · capital Havana</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Eleven months of British rule over Havana during the Seven Years' War, which opened Cuba to trade and spurred sweeping Spanish reforms.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> entered the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seven-years-war">Seven Years' War</a>, a British expedition under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/george-keppel-3rd-earl-of-albemarle">George Keppel</a>, Earl of Albemarle, and Admiral Sir <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/george-pocock">George Pocock</a> landed near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/havana">Havana</a> in June 1762. After a siege in which the defender <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/luis-vicente-de-velasco">Luis Vicente de Velasco</a> died at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/morro-castle-havana">Morro Castle</a>, the city surrendered on 13 August 1762, with much of the Spanish fleet.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/george-keppel-3rd-earl-of-albemarle">Albemarle</a> governed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/havana">Havana</a> and its surroundings as military governor, levying heavy contributions on the city. British merchants poured in, trade with the British colonies opened, and several thousand enslaved Africans were imported, a sign of the later growth of Cuban <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sugar-plantation">sugar</a>. Catholic institutions continued, though friction with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishop</a> led to his expulsion. Disease killed many British soldiers.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-paris-1763">Treaty of Paris</a> in 1763 <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/united-kingdom">Britain</a> returned <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/havana">Havana</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> in exchange for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/florida">Florida</a>. Shocked by the loss, Spain fortified the city with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/la-cabana">La Cabaña</a> fortress, reorganised its colonial militias and began liberalising Cuban trade as part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bourbon-reforms">Bourbon reforms</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/british-occupation-of-havana.jpg" alt="British occupation of Havana" width="320" height="219"><figcaption>Image: Dominic Serres · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Dominic_Serres_-_The_Capture_of_Havana,_1762,_Taking_the_Town,_14_August.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1762 CE – 1763 CE</dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>colony</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>colonial</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Havana</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>George Keppel, 3rd Earl of Albemarle</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Catholicism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Spanish and English</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd>Cuba</dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>British Havana</dd></dl></aside></div>
